 ## Overview
-Bitmask Leaf Compression is a memory optimization technique for Sparse Voxel Octrees that eliminates leaf nodes at the deepest level by packing their voxel data into a single integer field within their parent node [S1, unofficial]. It solves the excessive memory overhead of naive octree representations, where storing explicit leaf nodes causes memory usage to approach 8 times that of a full octree of depth $d-1$ [S1, unofficial]. By removing the leaf node structure entirely, it reduces the memory cost factor $mc$ from 8 to 1 for the deepest level [S1, unofficial].
+Bitmask Leaf Compression is a memory optimization technique for Sparse Voxel Octrees that eliminates leaf nodes at the deepest level by packing their voxel data into a single integer field within their parent node [S1, unofficial]. It solves the excessive memory overhead of naive octree representations, where storing explicit leaf nodes causes the memory cost factor $mc$ to approach 8 for deep trees [S1, unofficial]. By removing the leaf node structure entirely, it reduces the memory cost factor $mc$ from 8 to 1 for the deepest level [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A Sparse Voxel Octree with a defined maximum depth $d$.
-**Outputs:** An optimized octree structure where the penultimate level nodes contain packed leaf data.
+**Inputs and Outputs:** Input is an octree node at depth $d-1$ with 8 child pointers [S1, unofficial]. Output is a modified node with its `leafs` field populated and the depth $d$ child nodes removed [S1, unofficial].
 
